A classic, deeply savory beef stew cooked with carrots, celery, potatoes, tomatoes, and red wine. Thickened with a classic butter-flour roux paste stirred in toward the end of cooking for a glossy, luscious gravy.

*Adapted from [Robert Irvine / Food Network](http://www.foodnetwork.com/recipes/robert-irvine/caribou-stew-recipe).*

## Ingredients

- 1 to 1.5 lbs stewing beef (or venison/caribou), cut into 1-inch cubes
- 4 large carrots, peeled and medium-diced
- 2 large onions, peeled and medium-diced
- 3 stalks celery, cleaned and medium-diced
- 2 large potatoes, peeled and medium-diced
- 3 large tomatoes, diced (or 1 can 14.5 oz diced tomatoes)
- 3 cloves garlic, finely chopped
- 1 dried bay leaf
- 1 quart (4 cups) beef stock
- 1 ½ cups dry red wine (Cabernet Sauvignon or Pinot Noir, or substitute beef stock)
- ½ cup all-purpose flour
- 4 oz (1 stick) unsalted butter, melted
- 2 tablespoons finely chopped fresh thyme leaves
- 1 teaspoon finely chopped fresh rosemary leaves
- Kosher salt and freshly ground black pepper, to taste

## Instructions

1. **Simmer the Base:** In a large heavy pot or Dutch oven, combine the cubed meat, carrots, onions, celery, potatoes, tomatoes, minced garlic, and bay leaf. Pour in the beef stock and red wine. Bring to a boil over high heat, then reduce the heat to low, cover with a lid, and simmer gently for about 45 minutes.
2. **Whisk the Butter Roux:** In a small heatproof bowl, thoroughly whisk together the melted butter and all-purpose flour until a smooth, paste-like mixture forms.
3. **Thicken the Gravy:** Slowly stir the butter-flour mixture into the simmering stew. Add the chopped fresh thyme, rosemary, and generous pinches of salt and black pepper.
4. **Simmer to Tender:** Continue to simmer uncovered over medium-low heat, stirring occasionally to prevent sticking on the bottom, for about 30 minutes. The stew is ready when the meat is melt-in-your-mouth tender and the broth has reduced into a velvety, thick gravy.
5. **Serve:** Remove and discard the bay leaf. Taste and adjust seasoning with additional salt and black pepper as needed. Ladle into wide bowls and serve with crusty bread.

## Notes

- **Slow Cooker Instructions:** Place the meat, carrots, onions, celery, potatoes, tomatoes, garlic, bay leaf, beef stock, and wine into a 6-quart slow cooker. Cover and cook on LOW for 7 to 8 hours (or HIGH for 4 to 5 hours) until the meat is fork-tender. 30 minutes before serving, whisk together the melted butter and flour, stir into the slow cooker along with the fresh thyme and rosemary, cover, and cook on HIGH for 30 minutes until the gravy thickens.
- **Game Meats:** Venison, elk, or caribou make fantastic lean substitutions for beef in this stew.
